      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Extragenital endometriosis can occur in the rectum and sigmoid causing cyclic rectal bleeding. A hormonal intrauterine device (IUD) (20 microg/24 h levonorgestrel releasing), originally developed as an easily reversible contraceptive method, is a therapeutic option for bleeding disorders."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A 34-year-old woman using depot progesterone injection (crys-talline suspension of 150 mg medroxyprogesterone acetate) for contraception was amenorrheic and asymptomatic. After switching to a levonorgestrel-releasing IUD the patient experienced irregular bleeding with concomitant dysmenorrhea and rectal bleeding. Colonoscopy revealed a sigmoid mass. Laparotomy with resection of the sigmoidal mass and ovarian cyst was performed. Histopathologic analysis confirmed the suspected diagnosis of large bowel endometriosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "In our patient, large bowel endometriosis became symptomatic 2 years after insertion of hormonal IUD. The suppressive effect of the hormonal IUD seemed to be insufficient for the control of extragenital endometriosis."
        }
      ]},
